# pnpm configuration for Your HR
#
# Both settings below exist because the defaults are not portable or not
# always writable. Neither is macOS-specific.

# The pnpm store is a *user-level* cache, shared by every project on the
# machine. pnpm normally resolves it to ~/Library/pnpm/store (macOS) or
# ~/.local/share/pnpm/store (Linux) and reuses those cached tarballs.
#
# It only falls back to a *project-local* .pnpm-store when the package root
# cannot hardlink into $HOME. That fallback copies the entire content
# addressable store into the checkout, which duplicates roughly a gigabyte
# here and is the reason installs stall on a nearly-full disk.
#
# `${HOME}` is expanded by pnpm itself, so this stays correct for every
# developer and for CI runners. An absolute macOS path would not resolve on
# Linux CI and would fail the install outright.
#
# On macOS this points at pnpm's own store, so the existing cache is reused.
# On Linux CI the directory does not exist yet — pnpm creates it (verified),
# so the install works from a cold cache.
store-dir=${HOME}/Library/pnpm/store

# The metadata cache defaults to ~/Library/Caches/pnpm on macOS, which is not
# writable in every environment (sandboxed agents, locked-down CI images).
# Keep it inside the project instead so the value is portable. It is
# git-ignored.
cache-dir=.pnpm-cache

# Never run install scripts for dependencies. The project needs no native build
# steps, and this removes an entire class of supply-chain risk.
ignore-scripts=true
